have you ever wondered how people make  their photos look so good well today I'm  gonna teach you how to color great like  a professional what's going on guys my  name is Dave Thomas and welcome back to  the channel today we're going to look at  how you can color great your photos like  professional we're going to be using  Adobe Lightroom you can use it either on  your desktop or your mobile but today's  purposes we're gonna jump straight into  the desktop version and get started okay  so if you ever wondered how people turn  their photos from something like this  into something amazing like this it  actually isn't too hard and it doesn't  take much longer than maybe 2 or 3  minutes so for the purposes of this  video we're gonna take this photo back  to its original state this is how it was  shot it was shot against the Sun very  dark very underexposed and originally I  thought it was unusable but thankfully  this photo was shot in RAW which means  you can bring out a lot of the shadows  and highlights back into play so in  Adobe Lightroom you're gonna see this  panel on the right hand side this is  where your main functions are and this  is what you're gonna need to use the  most don't be intimidated by the fact  there's quite a few settings here it  gets very simple to use after a couple  of times of trying it out the first  thing you want to do is remove any  chromatic aberration and also enable  profile Corrections so with my camera it  picks up what camera it is and what lens  it is which makes it very easy and  efficient for me to do for every photo  time time again the second thing you  want to do with almost every photo is  tap the level correction in this case  it's moved a level a little bit too far  and we need to also take the crop  constraint but simply straight away you  can tell that the photo is now centered  its level with the horizon and there  doesn't need to be too many more  adjustments after this you want to  scroll to the top and get started on the  basic Corrections of the photo so as  we've already said this photo is  underexposed  and the highlights are blown out so  we're gonna remove the highlights  bringing them back to about maybe - 60 -  65 somewhere there and bringing the  shadows up as much as we can without  overdoing it if you go to 100 you're  gonna notice that it starts to get a bit  too  and he loses a lot of the clarity in the  blacks so we're gonna bring it back down  to maybe 70 70 percent in the positive  and because we can see straight away  that bringing up the shadows has  destroyed the contrast and destroyed the  blacks we're gonna drop the blacks down  a little bit lower to try get some more  deep rich colors in this photo and also  bring up the contrast a little bit more  to make it a bit punchier if you've  never used an s-curve before it's a very  simple process to use down the bottom  here is all your shadows this section  here is your mid-tones and at the very  top is your highlights  so as you adjust each section is how its  gonna change that particular part of the  photo I always start with a three-point  s-curve so for me it's three points  across the middle starting with the  shadows at the bottom the shadows  usually are bumped up a little bit just  to bring out that extra little bit  that's been lost in the details and then  dropping the blacks down a little bit  more so it starts to create that S shape  I normally don't touch too many of the  mid-tones because this is usually around  the face and the skin tones in most of  my photography but I do play with the  highlights a little bit as well so if we  bring the highlights down you're gonna  notice that the sky comes into clarity a  bit more and that we were able to remove  the harshness for the Sun and that's  pretty much all you have it for my s  curve in this photo you can also drop it  down and go to the red-green-blue  filters which gives you different  overlays and for example if we put a red  filter in you start to get those deep  blues and the shadows which is a very  trendy theme on Instagram at the moment  all the Reds coming through in the faces  if you're looking for the cinematography  a little bit aged shot you can go that  as well but personally I don't like them  at all so I don't add any reds greens or  blues into the photo itself now this is  where the fun starts you really can get  creative with the colors and the  saturation in your photo so for me I  personally always start with saturation  and try to make the mood of the photo  work to the best of my advantage  in this one you can tell the reds are  mainly in my face and and a little bit  in the skintone so we don't buff them up  too much I always have a tendency to  move my sliders up to maximum just to  understand where the colors are in that  photo and how much I really want to move  them up so in this one you can tell that  it's in the orange and the trees and  also in my face so we pump it up a  little bit to about 30% same as the  yellows I think they're prominently  coming out and this little Sun flash  straight down the middle of the photo so  we're going to bump it up to maybe about  10% not much more than that the greens  is always the trees usually but it isn't  really bringing out too much accepting  the Sun flare so we're not going to  bring that one up pretty much at all if  you're using the desktop version a good  trick is just double click on the slider  or the name of the color itself to reset  it without having to try line it back up  with the zero the actors in this photo  are going to be the start of the ocean  and the Blues are gonna be the same  merging into the sky we don't want to  overdo it  here so we're gonna just leave the blues  and aquas about 20% the purples is  something I always have a tendency to  remove almost to the bottom usually in  between fifty and a hundred percent in  the minus side because in my camera I  found that it's predominantly the  chromatic aberration in my photo so a  tendency to remove my purples and  magentas as much as possible  now if we move over to the hues of the  photo from memory that the Reds the  orange and the yellows are the primary  colors in this photo and the blue is in  the background so if we want to make the  blues a little bit more of a teal color  just to give it more of a vibrant pop  and the greens a little bit more vibrant  as well whereas the yellows and the  oranges we're not gonna move too much  but you can tell that in the face it is  a little bit too orange so we're gonna  move it slightly to the yellow side to  try to get those natural skin colors  back into play so only for in the Plus  on the oranges all it takes in this  photo split toning is one of the things  that you don't need to do in any  standard photo grading but it is  something that you'll see in most  professional level photo edits so adding  a hue to your highlights and your  shadows is very similar to using the s  but you have a bit more control in your  colors so to really understand what it  does we're going to pump it up to about  40% and slide the hue across because  this photo is taken outside near the  ocean I really want to reflect those  blues into the sky and into the  highlights a little bit more but  obviously not having it at such a high  saturation so putting a tiny little bit  of blue at about 10% is perfect for this  photo whereas the shadows we want to add  a little bit more orange to get a bit  more vibrance of the Sun coming through  now we can see that this photo is pretty  crisp there's a little bit of noise  coming through so we're only gonna add a  little bit of luminance to take away the  noise and they can even shop a photo now  obviously these photos for most people  will be going up on Instagram so the  last thing that we need to do is do the  crop overlay go original 4 by 5 which is  the Instagram crop filter and then  adjust at how you want it and that's all  there is to it guys so in less than 5  minutes we've managed to take a photo  from almost unusable to an Instagram  banger there really isn't much more to  it than that guys you can apply the same  principle to almost any photo you have  on your phone or straight off your DSLR  camera if you enjoyed this video make  sure you smash that subscribe button  20-19 style and I'll see you next Monday  for a brand new econ video

